Data refers to raw facts and figures that have not yet been processed or organized. It can represent text, numbers, images, sounds, or any other type of information. For example, "John", "25", and "Engineer" are pieces of data. A database is an organized collection of data that is stored and accessed electronically. It allows for efficient storage, retrieval, and management of data. Databases can be used to store anything from customer records to financial transactions. A relational database is a type of database that stores data in a structured format using tables. Each table consists of rows and columns, and relationships between tables are defined through keys (like primary and foreign keys). It follows the principles of relational algebra. Examples include MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Oracle. A table is a basic structure in a relational database where data is stored. It is made up of rows (also called records) and columns (also called fields or attributes). Each row represents a single data item, and each column represents a specific property of that item.
